e5-linux: do not let netifd's modemmanager proto flight-mode the modem when
it takes wan down.  Applied to the package directory (net/modemmanager) by
openwrt/build-modemmanager.sh.

The E5's data interface is a Unisoc SIPC netdev (sipa_eth0) whose carrier the
baseband releases on its own: measured here, SIPA_RM_RES_CONS_WWAN_DL/UL drops
2->0, netifd sees the link loss, and the interface teardown used to run
`mmcli --disable` as its last step.  That leaves the modem registered with
nothing: +CFUN? still 1, +COPS? still answers the operator, but ModemManager
reports state disabled with no 3GPP interface, and every status surface of the
hotspot then reads "searching for network" with no signal bars.  Losing a
bearer is not a reason to leave the network.

This is upstream's own switch, only its default changed: an interface can still
ask for the old behaviour with `option disable_modem '1'`.

--- a/files/lib/netifd/proto/modemmanager.sh
+++ b/files/lib/netifd/proto/modemmanager.sh
@@ -875,7 +875,7 @@ proto_mm_stop() {
 	mm_modem_set_roaming "${device}" ""
 
 	# disable modem
-	local disable="$(uci_get network "$interface" disable_modem "1")"
+	local disable="$(uci_get network "$interface" disable_modem "0")"
 	if [ "${disable}" -eq 0 ]; then
 		echo "Skipping modem disable"
 	else
